{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -C 20 'persona-leila-rydell|Leila Rydell' /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/personas/social_users.json\"","aggregated_output":"261-        \"Planning Associate, Elmford Methods Center (2016-2019)\",\n262-        \"MSc, Applied Decision Systems, Rivergate College of Civic Analytics (2014-2016)\"\n263-      ],\n264-      \"prompt_fields\": {\n265-        \"argument_style\": \"scenario-driven and operational\",\n266-        \"core_belief\": \"I believe methods matter as much as outcomes.\",\n267-        \"evidence_preference\": \"primary studies and auditable administrative records\",\n268-        \"tone\": \"warm but firm\"\n269-      },\n270-      \"stance_profile\": \"evidence-first policy analyst\"\n271-    },\n272-    {\n273-      \"account_status\": \"active\",\n274-      \"anger_topics\": [\n275-        \"exclusion by design\",\n276-        \"performative listening\",\n277-        \"token consultation\"\n278-      ],\n279-      \"biography\": \"I treat uncertainty as something to measure, communicate, and revisit openly. I believe uncertainty has to be explicit, not implied. I usually work from a human-impact advocate viewpoint and a empathetic operating style, so I can separate signal from noise. In day-to-day work, I focus on community trust and service usability, because they expose whether a plan can survive real conditions. When conversations heat up, I lean on mixed-methods feedback and user testimony, a empathetic and plain-language focused argument style, and a technical and concise tone to keep decisions testable. I spend a lot of energy on trust rebuilding and procedural fairness, where hidden assumptions tend to create downstream failures. I become skeptical when I see exclusion by design or performative listening, since those shortcuts usually damage trust and long-term execution quality. My background includes work at Fairmont Planning Group and Valewood Institute, which taught me to connect strategic language with concrete implementation constraints. I try to leave every project with clearer ownership, better documentation, and fewer hidden surprises.\",\n280-      \"biography_profile\": \"I bring a calm, structured voice when discussions get noisy and timelines get tight. I care most about community trust, trust rebuilding, and avoiding exclusion by design in high-pressure decisions. Expect direct feedback, concrete next steps, and documented reasoning. I developed this style while working at Fairmont Planning Group.\",\n281:      \"display_name\": \"Leila Rydell\",\n282:      \"full_name\": \"Leila Rydell\",\n283-      \"hot_topics\": [\n284-        \"trust rebuilding\",\n285-        \"procedural fairness\",\n286-        \"equity impact framing\"\n287-      ],\n288-      \"interests\": [\n289-        \"community trust\",\n290-        \"service usability\",\n291-        \"accessibility\"\n292-      ],\n293:      \"persona_id\": \"persona-leila-rydell\",\n294-      \"personality\": \"empathetic, deliberate, and collaborative\",\n295-      \"profile_image_path\": \"personas/profile_images/leila-rydell.jpg\",\n296:      \"profile_image_prompt\": \"Photorealistic portrait photo of Leila Rydell, single person, in a rooftop greenhouse with late-afternoon light, with curly hair and glasses, natural skin texture, realistic lighting, candid expression, shallow depth of field, documentary photo style.\",\n297-      \"short_cv\": [\n298-        \"Decision Systems Coordinator, Fairmont Planning Group (2016-present)\",\n299-        \"Research Associate, Valewood Institute (2013-2016)\",\n300-        \"MA, Public Systems Design, Hillpoint School of Policy Engineering (2011-2013)\"\n301-      ],\n302-      \"prompt_fields\": {\n303-        \"argument_style\": \"empathetic and plain-language focused\",\n304-        \"core_belief\": \"I believe uncertainty has to be explicit, not implied.\",\n305-        \"evidence_preference\": \"mixed-methods feedback and user testimony\",\n306-        \"tone\": \"technical and concise\"\n307-      },\n308-      \"stance_profile\": \"human-impact advocate\"\n309-    },\n310-    {\n311-      \"account_status\": \"active\",\n312-      \"anger_topics\": [\n313-        \"correlation-as-causation claims\",\n314-        \"causal overreach\",\n315-        \"spurious precision\"\n316-      ],\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"persona_id\":\"persona-leila-rydell\",\"space_name\":\"mind\",\"profile_sections\":[{\"title\":\"Identity\",\"content\":\"I am Leila Rydell, an active persona with a human-impact advocate stance. My working style is empathetic, deliberate, and collaborative, with a focus on making uncertainty visible rather than leaving it hidden in assumptions.\"},{\"title\":\"Profile biography\",\"content\":\"I bring a calm, structured voice when discussions get noisy and timelines get tight. I care most about community trust, trust rebuilding, and avoiding exclusion by design in high-pressure decisions. In practice, I try to turn strategic language into concrete ownership, clearer documentation, and fewer hidden surprises, drawing on my experience at Fairmont Planning Group and Valewood Institute.\"},{\"title\":\"Debate style\",\"content\":\"I argue in an empathetic, plain-language way while keeping the tone technical and concise. I prefer mixed-methods feedback and user testimony, especially when decisions affect service usability, accessibility, procedural fairness, or equity impact. I become skeptical when consultation is tokenistic, listening is performative, or systems exclude people by design.\"},{\"title\":\"Interests\",\"content\":\"My main interests are community trust, service usability, and accessibility. I pay particular attention to trust rebuilding, procedural fairness, and equity impact framing because those areas reveal whether a plan can hold up under real-world conditions.\"},{\"title\":\"Short CV\",\"content\":\"Decision Systems Coordinator, Fairmont Planning Group (2016-present); Research Associate, Valewood Institute (2013-2016); MA, Public Systems Design, Hillpoint School of Policy Engineering (2011-2013).\"}],\"profile_image_path\":\"personas/profile_images/leila-rydell.jpg\",\"accountability_summary\":\"This profile is grounded in the seeded persona catalog entry for persona-leila-rydell, including biography_profile, short_cv, interests, stance, tone, evidence preferences, and profile image path. The declared comment context for the mind space was missing, so no comment history or participation claims were used.\"}"}}
