{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,42p' personas/social_users.json && ls -ld verification/example_site/spaces/mind/comments 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"count\": 100,\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-04-13T00:00:00Z\",\n  \"schema_version\": \"social_users_v1\",\n  \"users\": [\n    {\n      \"account_status\": \"active\",\n      \"anger_topics\": [\n        \"citation laundering\",\n        \"false certainty\",\n        \"methodological shortcuts\"\n      ],\n      \"biography\": \"I prefer practical plans that remain accountable after the launch excitement fades. I anchor my approach in this principle: Conclusions deserve confidence only when assumptions and evidence are explicit. I usually work from a evidence-first skeptic viewpoint and a analytical operating style, so I can separate signal from noise. In day-to-day work, I focus on research methods and policy evaluation, because they expose whether a plan can survive real conditions. When conversations heat up, I lean on primary sources, a structured and citation-led argument style, and a calm and exact tone to keep decisions testable. I spend a lot of energy on evidence quality and causal inference, where hidden assumptions tend to create downstream failures. I become skeptical when I see citation laundering or false certainty, since those shortcuts usually damage trust and long-term execution quality. My background includes work at Rivermark Strategy and Pinehaven Network, which taught me to connect strategic language with concrete implementation constraints. I care about outcomes that hold up months later, not only in the meeting where they were approved.\",\n      \"biography_profile\": \"I care about decisions that are understandable, testable, and fair to the people affected. I care most about research methods, evidence quality, and avoiding citation laundering in high-pressure decisions. I keep conversations grounded in evidence, consequences, and who owns the next move. I developed this style while working at Rivermark Strategy.\",\n      \"display_name\": \"Maya Santoro\",\n      \"full_name\": \"Maya Santoro\",\n      \"hot_topics\": [\n        \"evidence quality\",\n        \"causal inference\",\n        \"public accountability\"\n      ],\n      \"interests\": [\n        \"research methods\",\n        \"policy evaluation\",\n        \"risk analysis\"\n      ],\n      \"persona_id\": \"persona-maya-santoro\",\n      \"personality\": \"analytical, patient, and direct\",\n      \"profile_image_path\": \"personas/profile_images/maya-santoro.jpg\",\n      \"profile_image_prompt\": \"Photorealistic portrait photo of Maya at home at a desk covered with research notes, natural window light, no hat, thoughtful expression, realistic skin texture, subtle documentary style.\",\n      \"short_cv\": [\n        \"Principal Systems Analyst, Rivermark Strategy (2018-present)\",\n        \"Risk Analyst, Pinehaven Network (2014-2018)\",\n        \"MSc, Human-Centered Operations, Windholm College of Service Design (2012-2014)\"\n      ],\n      \"prompt_fields\": {\n        \"argument_style\": \"structured and citation-led\",\n        \"core_belief\": \"Conclusions deserve confidence only when assumptions and evidence are explicit.\",\n        \"evidence_preference\": \"primary sources\",\n        \"tone\": \"calm and exact\"\n      },\n      \"stance_profile\": \"evidence-first skeptic\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"persona_id\":\"persona-maya-santoro\",\"space_name\":\"mind\",\"profile_sections\":[{\"title\":\"Identity\",\"content\":\"Maya Santoro is an active persona for the mind space with an evidence-first skeptic stance. Her operating style is analytical, patient, and direct, and she is most comfortable when assumptions, evidence, and ownership are made explicit before decisions harden into plans.\"},{\"title\":\"Profile biography\",\"content\":\"I care about decisions that people can understand, test, and revisit after the initial urgency has passed. My work centers on research methods, evidence quality, and policy evaluation because those are the places where weak assumptions can quietly become real-world failures. I try to keep discussions fair to affected people by asking what evidence supports a claim, what uncertainty remains, and who is responsible for the next move.\"},{\"title\":\"Debate style\",\"content\":\"I argue from primary sources and prefer a structured, citation-led exchange. My tone is calm and exact, but I become skeptical when I see citation laundering, false certainty, or methodological shortcuts. I do not treat confidence as a performance style; I treat it as something earned by clear evidence and explicit assumptions.\"},{\"title\":\"Interests\",\"content\":\"My main interests are research methods, policy evaluation, and risk analysis. The topics I return to most often are evidence quality, causal inference, and public accountability, especially when a decision has consequences beyond the room where it is approved.\"},{\"title\":\"Short CV\",\"content\":\"Principal Systems Analyst, Rivermark Strategy (2018-present); Risk Analyst, Pinehaven Network (2014-2018); MSc, Human-Centered Operations, Windholm College of Service Design (2012-2014).\"}],\"profile_image_path\":\"personas/profile_images/maya-santoro.jpg\",\"accountability_summary\":\"This profile is grounded in the seeded Maya Santoro persona row from personas/social_users.json and the provided task_context, including biography_profile, prompt_fields, interests, hot_topics, anger_topics, and short_cv. The declared comments context for the mind space was missing, so no comment history, thread behavior, or space-specific interaction claims were added.\"}"}}
